From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: subarr-subgen <subarr@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 00:02:07 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] 0046: close the last runtime-mutation vector, --install

Patch 0044 stopped this image replacing its own patched subgen.py at container
start. It deliberately left one path open: --install downloaded requirements.txt
from McCloudS/subgen main and pip-installed it over the baked dependency set.

That is the same class one step removed. It replaces what the patched code RUNS
ON rather than the code itself, and it can move faster-whisper, ctranslate2 or
stable-ts out from under the baked SUBGEN_KWARGS and the CUDA build those were
chosen against.

It was never the footgun UPDATE was. UPDATE gets carried over verbatim from an
upstream compose file and fires on every start; reaching --install always took a
deliberate override of the image CMD. It is closed because the rule is worth
keeping whole rather than because it was dangerous: a fork that never mutates
itself at runtime is a rule you can actually check, and a rule with one
documented exception is where the next gap grows.

install_packages_from_requirements is left DEFINED but uncalled, so the
divergence from upstream stays a single deleted call site rather than fighting
every upstream edit to that function.

The startup notice now names --install alongside the other ignored inputs, and
its wording covers dependencies as well as code, since the previous text spoke
only about subgen.py.

Verified with a real control, made safe by PIP_REQUIRE_VIRTUALENV=true so the
download half still executes and is observable while nothing can install:
vanilla fetched requirements.txt (108 bytes), patched fetched nothing, warned
naming --install, left the sentinel subgen.py intact and exited 0. A pip freeze
fingerprint taken before and after was unchanged, proving the guard held.

The validator gate is now STRONGER than what it replaces. Instead of an
allow-list of three filenames, which could only catch the names it knew about
and let requirements.txt through because it was passed by variable rather than
as a literal, it asserts download_from_github has ZERO call sites, plus a second
gate that install_packages_from_requirements has zero call sites.
